ZERO 0.11 HOST INTEROPERABILITY CONTRACT

The current canonical compiler implements this contract. The frozen 0.9 release
and its preserved seed remain separate provenance. All new primitives are value operations introduced by ':'.

Name        Builtin Token Template Arity
host        57      557   86       0
argcount    58      558   87       0
argument    59      559   88       1
fileopen    60      560   89       2
fileread    61      561   90       3
filewrite   62      562   91       3
filesize    63      563   92       1
fileclose   64      564   93       1
filedelete  65      565   94       1
makedir     66      566   95       1
dirempty    67      567   96       1
filemove    68      568   97       2
hostalloc   69      569   98       1
hostprotect 70      570   99       3
hostfree    71      571   100      2
program     72      572   101      0
filesync    73      573   102      1
hostwait    74      574   103      1

:host[] returns 1 on Linux x64 ELF, 2 on Windows x64 PE, and 0 on all
other targets. :argcount[] counts user arguments (excluding executable name).
:argument[index] uses one-based user indexing and returns a persistent pointer
to a NUL-terminated argument, or 0 when the index is invalid. Windows arguments
are converted from its native wide command line to UTF-8. Linux preserves
argument bytes supplied by the operating system. Pointers have process lifetime
and must not be overwritten. No new source string grammar is introduced.

:fileopen[path | mode] returns an opaque handle or u64(-1) on any failure:
  0 read existing
  1 exclusive create, write only
  2 create or truncate, write only
  3 read/write existing
  4 exclusive create, write only, executable output
Linux create modes 1/2 use 0600 and executable mode 4 uses 0700, reduced by
umask; Windows applies its normal file ACL. Existing mode 2 file permissions
remain unchanged. No automatic parent-directory creation is implied.
Paths are NUL-terminated, nonzero, at most 4095 bytes. Linux passes filesystem
bytes directly; Windows validates UTF-8 and converts to its wide OS API.
Both backends require a regular file and reject a final symlink/reparse point.
Linux uses O_NONBLOCK so a FIFO probe does not wait for a peer; it validates
fstat type before any mode 2 truncation. Windows validates the opened handle
before truncation. These checks do not prevent ancestor-directory links. No sandbox or race-free path ownership is claimed.

:fileread[handle | buffer | count] returns bytes copied, 0 at EOF, or u64(-1).
:filewrite[handle | buffer | count] returns bytes written or u64(-1).
Partial transfers are visible; callers loop as appropriate. Linux read/write
retry EINTR. Valid owned buffers and arithmetic bounds remain caller duties.
:filesize[handle] returns the file size without changing the current position,
or u64(-1). :fileclose[handle] returns 0 or u64(-1). A failed Linux close is not
retried because the descriptor may already have been released.

:filesync[handle] requests synchronization of the opened file through native
Linux fsync or Windows FlushFileBuffers, returning 0 or u64(-1). Linux retries
EINTR and rejects descriptor values above INT_MAX instead of truncating them.
Use an owned writable regular-file handle for portable success. The operation
does not close or reposition the handle. A successful request does not prove
physical power-loss safety and does not flush parent directories, publish
renames durably, authenticate contents or turn multiple writes into a
transaction. Failed sync must be reported before claiming a completed update.

:hostwait[milliseconds] suspends the calling native process thread for a
bounded interval0..86400000 inclusive and returns0; larger unsigned values
return u64(-1) without sleeping. Linux uses nanosleep and retries EINTR using
the kernel-provided remaining timespec; other errors return u64(-1). Windows
uses Sleep. Scheduling can lengthen the delay; this is not a clock or deadline
guarantee. Zero is permitted and may yield to the native scheduler. The call
does not switch Zero cooperative tasks, register a timer or create a daemon.

:filedelete[path] removes a file; :makedir[path] creates one new directory;
:dirempty[path] removes only an empty directory. Existing mkdir destinations,
missing removal targets, wrong target types and access failures return u64(-1).
:filemove[source | destination] atomically renames on the same filesystem
without replacing any existing destination. Cross-filesystem moves fail; no
copy/delete fallback is performed. These operations return 0 on success and
u64(-1) on error. Errors are deliberately normalized; callers cannot infer
"missing" from -1 alone, since access and other failures share that result.

Native compiler-launcher memory operations:
:hostalloc[bytes] returns an OS-owned, page-aligned RW allocation or 0.
:hostprotect[address | bytes | execute] changes owned allocation pages to RX
when execute is 1, RW when 0; other values are rejected. Returns 0 or u64(-1).
It never requests RWX.
:hostfree[address | bytes] releases the owned mapping, returning 0 or u64(-1).
The caller must track allocation size, protect only owned complete pages, and
never free an allocation still used by code/data/stack. Protection and release
are explicit; ordinary Zero pointers do not imply ownership. Backend limits
and platform rounding are documented separately.
:program[] returns a persistent NUL-terminated absolute executable path, or 0.
Linux consumes a fresh 4096-byte arena allocation per successful invocation;
Windows uses a stable startup buffer. Call once and cache the result. Linux reads its own process executable link; Windows obtains
its module path via the OS wide API and converts it to UTF-8. Failure is not
silently replaced by argv0, which can be relative or untrusted.
The existing :call[fn | a1 | a2 | a3 | a4 | a5] primitive is enabled only for
--host and --windows, in addition to its existing firmware/kernel targets.
It uses the Microsoft x64 ABI, including five arguments and shadow space, to
call owned loaded ZEROAPP1 code. Linux native OS/library functions do not
implicitly use this ABI. This is an unsafe explicit call, not process isolation.

Guest --app64/--app32 builds accept the new source forms. host, argcount and
argument, hostalloc and program return 0; file operations, hostprotect,
hostfree and hostwait return u64(-1), after evaluating operands.
They perform no filesystem access or foreign calls. This permits a shared
Ezero runtime to compile for guests while reporting unavailable capabilities.
Raw/EFI/real targets accept only host[] from this extension; other host APIs
are rejected. All names remain reserved in every target.

EMISSION ABI
All expressions evaluate left to right. x64 unary argument is RAX; binary
arguments are first RCX, second RAX (common parser consumes the saved first
operand). Ternary arguments are all pushed: stack top count, then buffer, then
handle. Each x64 argument occupies 8 bytes; the template consumes all 24 bytes
and returns in RAX. Preserve RBP, RSP after operand consumption, and R12..R15.
IA32 guest fallbacks obey the existing full-u64 paired-register ABI: binary
first ECX/EBX, second EAX/EDX; each pushed u64 consumes 8 bytes. Return EDX:EAX.
The real-mode host[] constant uses the existing EAX u32 value contract.

The original hosted frame stores argc at [RBP], argv0 at [RBP+8], and first
user argument at [RBP+16]. New host prologues retain its address in R14; argument
operations use that stable original frame rather than a fork child RBP. Existing
fork/switch templates preserve R14. Windows keeps R15 for its PE image base.
Backend templates reject index 0, index>=argc, and arithmetic overflow before
dereferencing argv. The original main frame remains live for process lifetime.

Compiler decoded-template storage grows from 8192 to 32768 bytes.
Only following scratch/metadata positions and reservation move by 24576:
scratch 3973184, metadata 3977880, reservation 4022880 inside the unchanged 4 MiB
arena. Output limit 491520, source limit 262144, and all original code prologues
remain unchanged unless a host backend explicitly needs a startup change.
